{"track":"calculus","num":"5.01","slug":"the-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus","title":"The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus","sections":[{"heading":"Section 1: FTC Part 1 (Derivative of an Accumulation Function)","questions":[{"q":"If $g(x) = \\int_1^x \\sqrt{t^3 + 1}\\,dt$, find $g'(x)$.","choices":["$\\sqrt{x^3 + 1}$","$\\frac{3x^2}{2\\sqrt{x^3 + 1}}$","$\\sqrt{x^3 + 1} - \\sqrt{2}$","$\\frac{1}{3}(x^3 + 1)^{3/2}$"],"correct":0,"answer":"By FTC Part 1, $\\frac{d}{dx}\\left[\\int_a^x f(t)\\,dt\\right] = f(x)$. Thus $g'(x) = \\sqrt{x^3 + 1}$."},{"q":"Find $\\frac{d}{dx}\\left[\\int_x^5 \\cos(t^2)\\,dt\\right]$.","choices":["$-\\cos(x^2)$","$\\cos(x^2)$","$-2x\\sin(x^2)$","$\\cos(25) - \\cos(x^2)$"],"correct":0,"answer":"Reverse limits: $\\int_x^5 = -\\int_5^x$. Differentiating yields $-\\cos(x^2)$."},{"q":"Find $h'(x)$ for $h(x) = \\int_0^{x^2} \\sin(t)\\,dt$.","choices":["$2x\\sin(x^2)$","$\\sin(x^2)$","$2x\\cos(x^2)$","$\\sin(x^4)$"],"correct":0,"answer":"Chain rule with FTC 1: $\\frac{d}{dx}\\left[\\int_a^{u(x)} f(t)\\,dt\\right] = f(u(x)) \\cdot u'(x) = \\sin(x^2) \\cdot 2x = 2x\\sin(x^2)$."}]},{"heading":"Section 2: FTC Part 1 with Variable Limits on Both Bounds","questions":[{"q":"Find $F'(x)$ for $F(x) = \\int_{2x}^{3x} (t^2 + 1)\\,dt$.","choices":["$27x^2 + 3 - 2(4x^2 + 1) = 19x^2 + 1$","$19x^2 + 1$","$27x^2 - 8x^2 = 19x^2$","$5x^2 + 1$"],"correct":0,"answer":"Split at 0: $\\int_{2x}^{3x} = \\int_0^{3x} - \\int_0^{2x}$. Differentiating: $3[(3x)^2 + 1] - 2[(2x)^2 + 1] = 3(9x^2 + 1) - 2(4x^2 + 1) = 27x^2 + 3 - 8x^2 - 2 = 19x^2 + 1$."},{"q":"Evaluate $\\frac{d}{dx}\\left[\\int_{\\sin x}^{\\cos x} e^t\\,dt\\right]$.","choices":["$-e^{\\cos x}\\sin x - e^{\\sin x}\\cos x$","$e^{\\cos x} - e^{\\sin x}$","$e^{\\cos x}\\sin x + e^{\\sin x}\\cos x$","$-e^{\\cos x} + e^{\\sin x}$"],"correct":0,"answer":"FTC with chain rule: $e^{\\cos x} \\cdot (-\\sin x) - e^{\\sin x} \\cdot (\\cos x) = -e^{\\cos x}\\sin x - e^{\\sin x}\\cos x$."},{"q":"Find the inflection points of $g(x) = \\int_0^x (t^3 - 3t)\\,dt$.","choices":["$x = 1$ and $x = -1$","$x = 0$ and $x = \\pm\\sqrt{3}$","$x = 3$","No inflection points"],"correct":0,"answer":"$g'(x) = x^3 - 3x$. $g''(x) = 3x^2 - 3 = 3(x-1)(x+1) = 0 \\Rightarrow x = \\pm 1$. Sign of $g''(x)$ changes at $x = -1$ and $x = 1$."}]},{"heading":"Section 3: FTC Part 2 (Evaluating Definite Integrals)","questions":[{"q":"Evaluate $\\int_1^3 (3x^2 - 4x + 1)\\,dx$.","choices":["$12$","$14$","$10$","$16$"],"correct":0,"answer":"Antiderivative $F(x) = x^3 - 2x^2 + x$. $F(3) = 27 - 18 + 3 = 12$. $F(1) = 1 - 2 + 1 = 0$. Value $= 12 - 0 = 12$."},{"q":"Evaluate $\\int_0^{\\pi/4} \\sec^2(x)\\,dx$.","choices":["$1$","$\\sqrt{2}$","$1/2$","$0$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$[\\tan x]_0^{\\pi/4} = \\tan(\\pi/4) - \\tan(0) = 1 - 0 = 1$."},{"q":"Evaluate $\\int_1^e \\frac{4}{x}\\,dx$.","choices":["$4$","$4e$","$4\\ln(e) - 4 = 0$","$1$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$[4\\ln|x|]_1^e = 4\\ln(e) - 4\\ln(1) = 4(1) - 4(0) = 4$."}]},{"heading":"Section 4: Average Value of a Function","questions":[{"q":"State the formula for the average value $f_{\\text{avg}}$ of a continuous function $f$ on $[a, b]$.","choices":["$f_{\\text{avg}} = \\frac{1}{b - a} \\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx$","$f_{\\text{avg}} = \\frac{f(a) + f(b)}{2}$","$f_{\\text{avg}} = \\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx$","$f_{\\text{avg}} = \\frac{f(b) - f(a)}{b - a}$"],"correct":0,"answer":"The average value is the total accumulation divided by the width of the interval: $\\frac{1}{b - a}\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx$."},{"q":"Find the average value of $f(x) = 3x^2 - 2x$ on the interval $[0, 2]$.","choices":["$2$","$4$","$3$","$1$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\frac{1}{2 - 0} \\int_0^2 (3x^2 - 2x)\\,dx = \\frac{1}{2}[x^3 - x^2]_0^2 = \\frac{1}{2}[8 - 4] = \\frac{1}{2}(4) = 2$."},{"q":"By the Mean Value Theorem for Integrals, find $c \\in [0, 3]$ such that $f(c) = f_{\\text{avg}}$ for $f(x) = 2x$.","choices":["$1.5$","$1.0$","$2.0$","$0.75$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$f_{\\text{avg}} = \\frac{1}{3}\\int_0^3 2x\\,dx = \\frac{1}{3}[x^2]_0^3 = \\frac{1}{3}(9) = 3$. Set $2c = 3 \\Rightarrow c = 1.5$."}]}]}